WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.680
A floor plate and a piece of dorsal spinal cord sit apart in a gel.

2
00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:09.800
How could one steer the other?

3
00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:15.340
Start on the map: commissural axons grow from the dorsal cord toward the floor

4
00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:16.340
plate.

5
00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:22.320
In 1988, in Thomas Jessell's laboratory, Marc Tessier-Lavigne and colleagues tested

6
00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:23.320
this in a dish.

7
00:00:24.083 --> 00:00:26.823
They cut pieces of embryonic rat spinal cord.

8
00:00:27.703 --> 00:00:31.323
Each piece was an explant, living tissue cultured outside the embryo.

9
00:00:32.467 --> 00:00:34.647
They set the explants in a collagen gel.

10
00:00:35.507 --> 00:00:38.227
Some held dorsal spinal cord, others floor plate.

11
00:00:39.267 --> 00:00:40.507
First, the control.

12
00:00:41.387 --> 00:00:43.887
A dorsal explant sits in the gel on its own.

13
00:00:44.707 --> 00:00:45.707
What should it do?

14
00:00:51.367 --> 00:00:54.007
On its own, it sent out few or no axons.

15
00:00:57.500 --> 00:00:59.720
Now place a floor plate explant nearby.

16
00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:05.340
Bundles of commissural axons grew out of the dorsal explant, toward the floor plate.

17
00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:07.840
The explants never touched.

18
00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:10.180
So the effect had to cross the gel.

19
00:01:11.183 --> 00:01:15.063
A cue that draws axons from a distance is a chemoattractant.

20
00:01:15.763 --> 00:01:18.403
Steering by a diffusing cue is chemotropism.

21
00:01:19.367 --> 00:01:21.587
Picture smelling bread from down the street.

22
00:01:22.467 --> 00:01:25.007
You know which way the bakery is, but not the recipe.

23
00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:30.270
Unlike bread, whether netrin draws you in depends on who is smelling it.

24
00:01:31.183 --> 00:01:33.783
To name the molecule, you must purify it.

25
00:01:34.523 --> 00:01:35.923
Floor plates are far too small.

26
00:01:36.867 --> 00:01:43.167
So in 1994, the laboratory kept rat explants as the detector and searched embryonic

27
00:01:43.167 --> 00:01:44.167
chick brain.

28
00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:47.030
Thousands of brains went into the work.

29
00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:51.230
The extract was separated over several chromatography columns.

30
00:01:52.283 --> 00:01:56.763
After each step, every fraction went back onto rat spinal-cord explants.

31
00:01:57.603 --> 00:01:59.863
The axons reported which fractions to keep.

32
00:02:00.833 --> 00:02:02.333
Two proteins came through.

33
00:02:03.193 --> 00:02:07.973
Their sequences were related to laminin, a matrix protein, and to a worm protein,

34
00:02:08.353 --> 00:02:09.353
UNC-6.

35
00:02:10.167 --> 00:02:13.287
The authors named them netrin-1 and netrin-2.

36
00:02:13.907 --> 00:02:15.547
Each promoted commissural outgrowth.

37
00:02:16.567 --> 00:02:19.807
Does promoting outgrowth prove that netrin steers axons?

38
00:02:20.787 --> 00:02:22.087
Think about what the assay scored.

39
00:02:28.117 --> 00:02:33.317
No. The assay scored whether axons grew out, not which way they pointed.

40
00:02:34.277 --> 00:02:35.437
Outgrowth is not direction.

41
00:02:38.967 --> 00:02:41.167
A companion paper supplied direction.

42
00:02:42.187 --> 00:02:44.287
Floor plate cells make netrin-1 RNA.

43
00:02:45.467 --> 00:02:51.047
And cells engineered to secrete netrin attracted commissural axons at a distance in

44
00:02:51.047 --> 00:02:52.047
the gel.

45
00:02:52.367 --> 00:02:56.807
The takeaway: netrin is what crosses the gel, and it can attract at a distance.
